<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upanishads</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Upanishads are philosophical texts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> that form the latest layer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the oldest of them composed from around 700 BCE in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Through dialogues between teachers and pupils they explore the identity of the self, atman, with the ultimate reality, brahman, and develop ideas of rebirth and liberation. They became the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> and of much later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upanishads.jpg" alt="Upanishads" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_and_DiscipleI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Upanishads are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts of the late Vedic and post-Vedic periods. They are the most recent addition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, whose earlier parts dealt with mantras, benedictions, rituals, ceremonies and sacrifices. The word Upaniṣad is understood as &quot;sitting near a teacher&quot;, from upa &quot;by&quot; and ni-ṣad &quot;sit down&quot;, though it first meant &quot;connection&quot; or &quot;equivalence&quot;. Most authors are unknown, but the early texts credit sages such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a>, Uddalaka Aruni and Shvetaketu, and women such as Maitreyi and Gargi take part in the dialogues.</p><p>Scholars are uncertain about when they were composed. The Indologist Patrick Olivelle holds that no dating of the early texts can be closer than a few centuries. The early Upanishads are generally placed in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the region of Kuru-Panchala and Kosala-Videha, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janaka">Janaka</a> of Videha features prominently in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Of 108 Upanishads known from the Muktika list, about a dozen are the oldest and are called the principal or mukhya Upanishads. The Brihadaranyaka and the Chandogya are the oldest of these. They were memorised and passed down orally for centuries. Each Upanishad is attached to one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>. New Upanishads continued to be composed into the modern era, and in 1908 four previously unknown ones were found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><p>Their central concern is the relation between ritual, cosmic realities and the human person, with Atman, the self, and Brahman, the ultimate reality, at the summit of the universe. The older texts attack ritual with increasing intensity: the Brihadaranyaka calls anyone who worships a divinity other than the self a domestic animal of the gods. The ideas are plural. Some texts are monistic, while the Katha Upanishad is dualistic and the Maitri inclines towards the dualism that grounds the Samkhya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoga">Yoga</a> schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The mukhya Upanishads, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a> and the Brahmasutra together form the three main sources of all schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>. Their varied teaching allowed divergent readings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a>, in the 8th century CE, wrote commentaries on eleven principal Upanishads and is linked with the non-dual Advaita school, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> (1017 to 1137 CE) read them as teaching qualified monism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a> (1199 to 1278 CE) as strongly theistic. Modern scholars differ on how far Shankara's views follow the texts.</p><p>Translations of the early 19th century drew the attention of Western readers. The German philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-schopenhauer">Arthur Schopenhauer</a> was deeply impressed and called them the most profitable and elevating reading possible in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upanishads” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ritual specialists, the Brahmins, developed elaborate sacrificial systems; then the <b>Upanishads</b>, composed from about the 8th century BCE, turned inward, asking about the nature of the self and of ultimate reality and teaching that the two are one.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The later Vedic texts, the Brahmanas, which explain the rituals, and the <b>Upanishads</b>, which explore the nature of the self and ultimate reality, show the language changing towards its classical form.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…and women sages and goddesses were known, though a wife was subordinate to her husband; in the later period women were relegated to more subordinate roles, while the texts still name them as herders, weavers and dyers, the woman warrior Vishpala and two female philosophers of the <b>Upanishads</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Most historians now see its roots in older Indian traditions, from the <b>Upanishads</b> and the Gita to the Tamil poets, while recognising that in the north saints and Sufis drew on a shared world of ideas and images.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The verse is cited throughout Hindu literature: in the Brahmanas and Srauta-sutras of the Vedic liturgy, in <b>Upanishads</b> such as the Brihadaranyaka, Shvetashvatara and Maitrayaniya, which treat it esoterically, and in the Mahabharata, the Harivamsa and the Manusmṛti.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gayatri-mantra">Gayatri Mantra</a></li><li><blockquote>The Panchala king Pravahana Jaivali appears in the <b>Upanishads</b> instructing Brahmins on the doctrine of rebirth, a sign of the court's intellectual standing.</blockquote><a href="/state/panchala">Panchala</a></li></ul></section>
